From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] shared/tinyusb: don't clear the CDC TX FIFO under an IN
 transfer when the host closes the port.

On an ESP32-S3 on its native USB, a host that closes the CDC port while
the board is still sending reboots it: "Guru Meditation Error: Core 1
panic'ed (Interrupt wdt timeout on CPU1)", with the USB interrupt
re-entering dcd_int_handler -> handle_epin_slave -> epin_write_tx_fifo
in TinyUSB's DWC2 driver (micropython-pydevices#68). Closing straight
after leaving raw REPL, while the banner is going out, did it 18 times in
20 from pyserial; mpremote, terminals and IDEs that close mid-reply hit it
too.

When the host drops DTR, mp_usbd_cdc.c's line-state callback calls
tud_cdc_n_write_clear() (added upstream in 8d3597ca5, "Fix CDC reconnect
stall and TX FIFO"). With TinyUSB 0.21 (patch 0014) the DWC2 driver runs
in slave mode with dedicated endpoint FIFOs, and the CDC TX stream hands
its software FIFO to the driver instead of copying into an endpoint
buffer (usbd_edpt_xfer_fifo, tusb.c stream_xfer). Each packet after the
first is pulled from that FIFO in the TX-FIFO-empty interrupt. If the
clear empties it mid-transfer, epin_write_tx_fifo() reads nothing,
DIEPTSIZ never counts down, the interrupt stays unmasked and asserted,
and the handler runs until the interrupt watchdog fires. TinyUSB 0.18,
which upstream v1.29.0 pins, copies each transfer into an endpoint
buffer, so a stock build doesn't show this.

The callback now skips the clear while the CDC IN endpoint is busy. The
FIFO is overwritable from the moment DTR drops, so what stays in it can't
block a later write, which is what the clear was for. Raising GAHBCFG's
TX-FIFO-empty level to "completely empty" doesn't help: set from the
REPL, the same close still panicked 19 times in 20.

Proven on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-7 (ESP32_GENERIC_S3 LCD_7,
native USB CDC on Windows), counting panics on its UART console. Each
trial enters raw REPL, runs print(time.ticks_ms()), sends Ctrl-B and
closes at once:

  | how the port is closed          | without | with this patch |
  |---------------------------------|---------|-----------------|
  | plain close (Windows drops DTR) | 18/20   | 0/40            |
  | drop RTS and DTR, then close    | 8/20    | 0/40            |
  | mpremote 1.28 exec, its own way | 6/20    | 0/40            |

With the patch, closing in the middle of a 3000-line print loop (0/40)
and while the board was blocked on a full TX FIFO (0/10) didn't panic
either, and the friendly REPL, a 14 KB paste-mode block, 4000 lines of
output read back whole and in order, and mpftp (exec, a 42 KB put with
hash, run) all worked. Back on the unpatched build, the plain close
panicked 19 times in 20.

The ESP32-S2 and ESP32-P4 build TinyUSB 0.21 the same way, in slave
mode (CFG_TUD_DWC2_DMA_ENABLE defaults to 0), so they share the path.

Drop this patch when upstream guards the clear or TinyUSB makes
tud_cdc_n_write_clear() safe under an in-flight transfer.

Based-on: micropython 0fd6c573e (tag v1.29.0)
---
 shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c
index 8018032d3..a5f60c873 100644
--- a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c
+++ b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c
@@ -192,7 +192,17 @@ void MICROPY_WRAP_TUD_CDC_LINE_STATE_CB(tud_cdc_line_state_cb)(uint8_t itf, bool
     } else {
         // Host has closed the cdc serial port. Discard pending TX data to
         // avoid a full FIFO blocking writes on the next connection.
-        tud_cdc_n_write_clear(itf);
+        //
+        // But not while an IN transfer is in flight: in slave mode the DWC2
+        // driver (ESP32-S2/S3/P4) pulls each packet straight from this FIFO
+        // in its interrupt, and finding it emptied mid-transfer it writes
+        // nothing, the TX-FIFO-empty interrupt stays asserted, and the
+        // handler re-enters until the interrupt watchdog resets the chip.
+        // With DTR low the FIFO is overwritable, so what stays in it can't
+        // block a later write either.
+        if (itf != 0 || !usbd_edpt_busy(TUD_OPT_RHPORT, USBD_CDC_EP_IN)) {
+            tud_cdc_n_write_clear(itf);
+        }
     }
     #endif
     #if MICROPY_HW_USB_CDC_DTR_RTS_BOOTLOADER
